  <h1>India’s Agriculture and Climate Change — Vulnerabilities and Adaptation</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Agriculture in India is highly climate-sensitive, with ~60% of cropped area dependent on monsoons. In 2024, Indian Express reported erratic rainfall, heatwaves, and floods worsening agrarian distress. For UPSC, this issue captures the intersection of climate change, economy, and rural governance.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Agriculture Share: ~18% of GDP, ~50% of workforce.<br/>•	Climate Events (2023–24):<br/>o	Heatwaves → wheat yield losses in north India.<br/>o	Erratic monsoon → delayed sowing of paddy and pulses.<br/>o	Floods in Assam and Bihar damaging crops.<br/>•	Economic Cost: Climate shocks reduce farm incomes by up to 25% (NITI Aayog).<br/>•	Food Security Link: Productivity shocks threaten PDS supplies.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Vulnerabilities<br/>1.	Rainfall Variability: Monsoon dependency makes crops risky.<br/>2.	Rising Temperatures: Heat stress reduces yields in wheat, rice.<br/>3.	Water Stress: Over-extraction of groundwater for irrigation.<br/>4.	Pest and Disease Outbreaks: Climate shifts expand pest ranges.<br/>5.	Small Farmers at Risk: Marginal farmers least resilient to shocks.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC, 2008): Includes National Mission on Sustainable Agriculture.<br/>•	Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY): Crop insurance scheme.<br/>•	Soil Health Cards: For balanced fertiliser use.<br/>•	Micro-Irrigation Fund: ₹5,000 crore for drip/sprinkler irrigation.<br/>•	National Adaptation Fund on Climate Change: For climate-resilient farming.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Awareness Growth: Climate risks now part of agri policy discourse.<br/>•	Insurance Coverage: 5 crore farmers enrolled in PMFBY (2023–24).<br/>•	Climate-Resilient Seeds: ICAR developing drought/heat-tolerant varieties.<br/>•	Digital Tools: Weather apps and advisories improving.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Insurance Issues: Low payouts, delays discourage farmers.<br/>•	Irrigation Gaps: Only ~50% net sown area irrigated.<br/>•	Knowledge Divide: Small farmers lack climate advisories.<br/>•	Policy Focus: Subsidies still favour rice–wheat rather than diverse crops.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Food Security: Climate-proof agriculture vital for PDS and NFSA.<br/>•	Economic Growth: Rural incomes key for consumption demand.<br/>•	Social Stability: Climate shocks linked to farmer suicides, protests.<br/>•	Global Role: India central to climate–food debates in Global South.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Agriculture & Environment): Climate impacts, adaptation.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Welfare delivery, resilience policies.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Climate change is the biggest threat to Indian agriculture.”<br/>o	“Food security depends on climate security.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Political economy of agriculture and climate justice.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Diversify Crops: Promote millets, pulses, oilseeds.<br/>2.	Expand Irrigation: Micro-irrigation and rainwater harvesting.<br/>3.	Strengthen Insurance: Faster, transparent payouts via digital tools.<br/>4.	Climate-Smart Farming: Agroforestry, organic, resilient varieties.<br/>5.	Farmer Awareness: ICT-based weather and advisory services.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s agriculture faces a climate emergency, demanding urgent adaptation. Policies must shift from short-term relief to long-term resilience. For UPSC aspirants, this issue shows how economy, climate, and governance converge in rural India.</p>
